[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m During the 2015-16 and 2016-17 seasons, Curry appeared in 36 of 38 games, and shot 47.1% from 3, taking more than 200 three pointers in 2015 and more than 300 in 2016 and 2017. He has one playoff game, where he shot 9 for 11 from three and became the first to take 200 three point shots in a game. On April 2, 2016, he became the first to take 500 three-point shots in a game. On the night he scored 11 three-pointers to lead the Golden State Warriors to a game 7 victory over the Cleveland Cavaliers, Curry had a game-high 51 three-pointers, a career-high 50 in the 2016 playoffs, and three in the fourth quarter. He had scored over 40 three-pointers in the third quarter six times and on four occasions in the fourth, including a season-high five and a game-high five on December 31, 2015. Curry is ranked second in career three-point percentage at 43.4 and is known as "Klay" Curry and "DPOY" Curry. He is credited with leading the Golden State Warriors to the 2016 NBA championship and making Golden State the number one seed in the 2016 playoffs. He is the third three-pointer in NBA history, behind Kobe Bryant and Ray Allen.
