[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 📍 STEP 1 - SAMPLE 0: ANSWER Action
[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 📝 Question: What did romans use to clean their teeth?
[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 🤔 Ambiguous: False
[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 🤖 Response: Tooth cleaning was a learned activity and was not a natural reflex. Primitive brushes (made from twig, reeds, and even withier) were used to scrape tartar off. They probably used chewed pieces of linen or knotted rope to stimulate the gums and ridges of their teeth. At one time the practice of massaging with salt may have been used. There are Greek vase paintings that depict the wealthy using bronze-sheathed toothpicks and tools called tartrove or tooth gnashers, small hook and finger-tremble tools. Groups of these tooth gnashers have been found in many burial sites. As people adopted a more Mediterranean and Asian diet, especially a diet rich in meat and dairy products, more tartar and root caries formed in their mouth. Less common brushes were made of horse hair. Professional tools were mentioned in literary references to priests of Isis in Egypt and in paintings at Herculaneum. The most successful toothbrush was a toothbrush made in the late 1500s or early 1600s by Duverger, a member of the French court of King Louis XIV. It was invented by Duverger in the early 1600s and was the world's first commercially marketed toothbrush. One of the earliest names for the toothbrush is the badger-bristle broom. With the invention of more efficient textile manufacturing and refinement of brush-making process, it is likely that they were influenced by the older and more developed quill pen and makebrush factories.
