[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 🤔 Ambiguous: False
[36m(main_task pid=3594473)[0m 🤖 Response: Toothbrushes were not widespread in Roman society, and the earliest evidence for the use of a toothbrush comes from the fifteenth century. Some Roman artifacts also show toothbrushes or similar use. The next oldest depiction of brushes is from Assyria, and was present in a fresco in the palace of King Ashurbanipal in 710 BC. The number of claims of the "world's oldest bristles" may be unclear, but the first history of the toothbrush is around 400 years ago in Japan. Evidence for herbal toothpowders dates back to 530 BC. These herbs where polished and ground in water. The idea that the Chinese had a toothbrush prior to the modern invention of the toothbrush is not quite as widely accepted as the 2006 discoveries of 5,300 year old quince cleaners and ten thousand year old teethcombings from Israel.
